1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
HBM holds the data. L2 holds temporary cached copies. SMs run the GPU kernels.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:18,000
A and B are still in HBM. Some copies of their data also remain in L2.

3
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:27,000
We want to displace those cached copies before timing the next GEMM. We do not delete A or B.

4
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:40,000
A GPU kernel reads a separate 512 MiB buffer. These reads replace earlier A/B copies in the 60 MiB L2.

5
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:50,000
The large buffer is unchanged. Its cached copies are clean. Only 4 KiB of checksum results are written.

6
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:02,000
The GEMM brings A/B back from HBM. Unrelated clean cache lines can be discarded without writeback.

7
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Rewind to the same A/B data. This time, the preparation reads AND modifies the unrelated buffer.

8
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Modified copies are newer than their HBM backing data. Some remain in L2 after preparation ends.

9
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Replacing those dirty lines generates writes to HBM alongside the GEMM reads. The GEMM does the same math.

10
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:50,000
HBM reads and GEMM instruction counts match. Write-based preparation produces many more HBM writes.

11
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Eviction removes cached copies, not the original data. Clean copies can be dropped; dirty copies need writeback.
